<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Veer Savarkar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vinayak Damodar Savarkar was an Indian politician and ideologue who developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> nationalist ideology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> in his 1923 pamphlet Essentials of Hindutva. As a student in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he was involved with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> House, and in 1910 he was arrested and deported to India, where he was sentenced to 50 years of imprisonment in the Cellular Jail in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andaman-and-nicobar-islands">Andaman and Nicobar Islands</a>. Released in 1924 after a series of mercy petitions, he was restricted to Ratnagiri district until 1937 and then led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-mahasabha">Hindu Mahasabha</a>, which endorsed the idea of India as a Hindu Rashtra. In 1948 he was tried over the conspiracy to assassinate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> but was acquitted for lack of sufficient evidence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar.jpg" alt="Vinayak Damodar Savarkar" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swatyantra_Vir_Savarkar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
